10:32:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Regular knuckle cracking has not been shown to cause arthritis. Multiple studies, including long-term observational research, have found no meaningful increase in hand osteoarthritis among people who crack their knuckles daily. The “pop” is usually gas bubbles rapidly forming/collapsing in joint fluid, not bones grinding together. A few caveats: - If cracking is painful, causes swelling, locking, instability, or reduced motion, don’t push through it—get it evaluated. - Forceful twisting or pulling can injure soft tissues, even if ordinary painless cracking is generally harmless. - Some small studies have suggested frequent cracking might be associated with temporary hand swelling or slightly weaker grip, but evidence is limited and it is not the same as arthritis. So, if it is painless and you are not aggressively forcing your joints, your added long-term arthritis risk from daily knuckle cracking appears to be essentially none.
