10:32:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s an extremely common warning, but it’s one of those health myths that stuck around because it sounds plausible. Researchers have specifically looked for more hand arthritis in habitual knuckle-crackers and generally have not found it. One famous example was a doctor who cracked the knuckles of only one hand for decades and found no arthritis difference between his hands—though that is more of an amusing anecdote than definitive evidence. Larger observational studies also have not shown a clear arthritis link. What *does* raise arthritis risk more reliably includes: - Aging and genetics - Prior joint injuries or fractures - Repetitive heavy hand loading or certain occupations/sports - Inflammatory arthritis conditions, such as rheumatoid arthritis - Obesity, especially for weight-bearing joints Your grandma and teachers were likely trying to discourage a habit they found noisy or annoying, not passing along a medically established fact. If your knuckle cracking is painless and gentle, you are not meaningfully “wearing out” your joints.
