10:33:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It may not be a coincidence that your uncle has arthritis after 30 years—but that does not mean the knuckle cracking caused it. Arthritis is common with age, and hand arthritis can be driven by genetics, old injuries, repetitive manual work, inflammatory diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis, gout, and other factors. Also, people sometimes crack or manipulate their joints *because* they already feel stiff or uncomfortable, so the arthritis process may have started before it was obvious. The key point is that when researchers compare groups of habitual knuckle-crackers with non-crackers, they have not found convincing evidence that cracking itself increases arthritis risk. Swollen hands are worth medical attention, especially if the swelling is persistent, symmetric in both hands, accompanied by morning stiffness lasting more than about 30–60 minutes, warmth/redness, or fatigue. Those features can suggest inflammatory arthritis, which is different from ordinary wear-and-tear osteoarthritis and benefits from early treatment. So his experience is real, but it cannot by itself identify the cause—and current evidence does not support blaming daily painless knuckle cracking.
