10:33:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily “wrong” in a broad practical sense—but the specific claim that ordinary, painless knuckle cracking causes arthritis is not well supported by current evidence. Doctors may advise stopping because: - It can irritate a joint or tendon if you are forcefully pulling/twisting it. - Some people crack because a joint is already painful, unstable, or inflamed. - It may be a habit that causes discomfort, social stress, or distracts from an underlying hand problem. - Medical advice is sometimes deliberately cautious when evidence is limited. Your doctor also knows your individual hands, history, exam findings, medications, injuries, and symptoms—none of which I do. If she told you to stop because you had pain, swelling, hypermobility, tendon issues, or arthritis signs, her advice may be very specifically appropriate for you. A useful middle ground: if your cracking is painless, gentle, and there is no swelling or loss of function, it is unlikely to be causing arthritis. But if you have hand symptoms—or your doctor gave you a personal reason to stop—ask her directly: “Are you concerned it causes arthritis, or is there something about my joints specifically that makes cracking a problem?”
