10:33:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The door-hinge analogy is intuitive, but a knuckle joint is not really being “popped open and shut” in the way a metal hinge is. When you crack a knuckle, you are usually briefly separating the joint surfaces enough to change pressure in the joint fluid; a gas bubble forms and produces the pop. The joint is not repeatedly scraping cartilage against cartilage. In fact, your knuckles already move through vastly more cycles each day from typing, gripping, opening things, exercising, etc.—often under much more load than a typical crack. Joints can wear down over time, but the main drivers are things like injury, inflammatory disease, genetics, age, and sustained heavy mechanical loading—not the sound of cavitation itself. If ordinary cracking were meaningfully wearing out hand joints, frequent crackers should show noticeably more hand arthritis in studies, and that pattern has not shown up. The caveat is force: if you have to wrench, twist, or pull hard to get a pop—or it hurts or leaves swelling—that can strain ligaments, tendons, or an already-irritated joint. In that situation, stopping is sensible. But painless, gentle cracking is not known to “wear down” the joint like a damaged hinge.
