10:35:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is not being held to an unusually high standard. The standard is the same one we should use for any health claim: before saying an everyday behavior *causes* a disease, look for evidence that people who do it have more of that disease, ideally after accounting for other explanations. Doctors sometimes give precautionary advice based on limited evidence when the possible downside is serious and the intervention is easy. But that is different from calling something a known cause. For knuckle cracking: - The proposed harm—arthritis—is a specific long-term disease outcome. - The behavior is common and easy to study. - Studies comparing habitual crackers with non-crackers have generally not found more hand arthritis in crackers. Back cracking and posture are also often oversimplified. Gentle self-stretching or occasional back popping is not the same as forceful manipulation of a painful or unstable spine; and “bad posture” is not a single proven cause of pain or degeneration in the way people often imply. So the consistent message is: avoid movements that hurt, forceful manipulation after injury or with instability, and seek care for persistent symptoms. But don’t label a common painless behavior as disease-causing without evidence.
